From f894ba6ff222215d3e0921e2fd44238ae8f0c056 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 19:25:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09758/11362] [outer-loop] Experiment tier moves to gpt-5.6-sol

Operator: "where do you find gpt-5.5? we should be using gpt-5.6-sol".

It came from three places, and they had drifted apart:

  1. 30-codex-fallback-20260610.conf sets AGENT_MODEL=gpt-5.5, and it
     loads after 10-gemini-routing.conf, so it won.
  2. DEFAULT_MODEL_BY_TYPE["codex"] here was "gpt-5.5".
  3. The planner prompt literally instructed the planner to emit
     `model: gpt-5.5` for formulaic-code tasks, so roadmaps kept
     regenerating it even where the env was right.

Planner, retro and audit were already on gpt-5.6-sol via their own
AGENT_MODEL_* variables. Only the experiment tier lagged, which meant a
single milestone called two different models depending on which tier
ran. That is worse than either choice alone: an experiment result and
the retrospective reading it were not produced by the same model, so a
capability difference between tiers reads as a finding.

Fixing 1 and 2 without 3 would have regressed at the next plan, since
the prompt is what puts the string into the roadmap. Milestone 569
already carries two tasks with `model: gpt-5.5` from that instruction.

Env change lands via a new drop-in, 60-model-gpt56sol-20260823.conf,
rather than an edit to 30-. That file documents the 2026-06-10 gemini
stall that made codex the default; rewriting its Environment line would
leave its comment describing a state that no longer exists. Later
drop-ins win, so 60- overrides it and the history stays readable.

Historical gpt-5.5 mentions elsewhere in this file are incident records
and stay as written, per never-prune.

Takes effect on the conductor's next restart; it is a long-lived --loop
process and keeps its environment until then. Not forcing one now: a
codex task is in flight and a restart would kill it.

